Subjective eye strain and mild retro-orbital ache from prolonged screen time without resting breaks
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Retro-orbital dull ache, Gritty eye sensation, Relief with visual rest

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-040
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe retro-orbital headache and visual discomfort requiring assessment to rule out acute angle-closure glaucoma or optic neuritis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe eye pain and transient visual blurring. Urgent clinical evaluation was mandated prior to the next in-hours period to assess pupillary reflexes, evaluate extraocular movements, and definitively exclude sight-threatening conditions like acute angle-closure glaucoma or increased intracranial pressure.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My eyes feel incredibly dry, heavy, and there's a dull ache right behind them.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a dull aching discomfort localized behind the orbits and a subjective feeling of heavy eyelids. Reports transient blurring of vision that recovers immediately with blinking. Notes a sensation of dry or gritty eyes. Describes an exacerbation of symptoms following extended, unbroken periods of digital device use today. Reports rapid symptom improvement with visual rest and gazing into the distance. Complete absence of persistent visual loss, flashes, floaters, or severe nausea.
Functional Status: Fully independent, but limiting reading/screen time.
Social/Safety Concerns: Occupational requirement for prolonged screen use.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, rubbing eyes frequently, no photophobic posturing.
Physical Exam: Ophthalmic examination reveals normal extraocular muscle movements without pain. Intact, symmetric pupillary light reflexes bilaterally. Diagnostic triad confirmed: retro-orbital dull ache, gritty eye sensation, and relief with visual rest. Normal uncorrected visual acuity to gross testing. Absence of ciliary flush, corneal clouding, or conjunctival purulence. Visual fields are full to confrontation.
Environmental Observations: Multi-monitor computer setup in a dimly lit room.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient not using regular lubricating eye drops.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subjective eye strain and mild retro-orbital ache from prolonged screen time without resting breaks (Computer Vision Syndrome) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal, reactive pupils (not fixed/mid-dilated), clear cornea, and lack of severe, deep aching pain accompanied by nausea and halos around lights.

Optic Neuritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Painless extraocular movements, normal visual acuity, and absence of a relative afferent pupillary defect.

Giant Cell Arteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient demographic, normal temporal artery palpation, and lack of jaw claudication or systemic symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute visual disturbances and retro-orbital pain, symptoms that share features with severe ophthalmic emergencies like acute glaucoma or optic neuritis. An urgent face-to-face assessment was clinically mandated to evaluate pupillary function and perform a gross visual exam to prevent potential irreversible visual loss. The examination safely confirmed the etiology as benign, fatigue-induced muscular and corneal strain, avoiding an unnecessary emergency department referral while addressing the patient's severe discomfort.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Recommendation for frequent use of preservative-free artificial tears. Advice on proper ambient lighting and screen distance.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement the "20-20-20 rule" by setting a desk timer: every 20 minutes, look at an object 20 feet away for exactly 20 seconds, physically relaxing the ciliary muscles and forcing a blink cycle to re-lubricate the cornea.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine optometry review if symptoms persist despite ergonomic corrections and regular breaks.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you experience sudden, persistent vision loss, extreme deep eye pain, see halos around lights, or develop severe nausea and vomiting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical digital eye strain; persistent changes in visual acuity require formal optometric evaluation.
Clinical Course: The ache and grittiness will resolve completely within hours of sustained visual rest and lubrication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if one eye becomes bright red with a severe headache, or if you begin seeing flashes of light or sudden new floaters.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: When staring at a screen, your blink rate drops by over 50%, causing the tear film to evaporate and the eyes to feel gritty. Additionally, the small muscles inside your eye spasm from focusing at a fixed, close distance for too long.
Actionable Steps: Use artificial tears every 2 hours while working. Ensure your room is well lit to avoid excessive glare from the screen. Practice the 20-20-20 rule strictly.

SMS INFORMATION

